          <toggle-section open="false">
            <h3>Video transcript </h3>
            <p><strong>Leah:</strong> For a product to be successfully used, a good ‘user interface’ [UI] is critical. Users won’t stay long and will quickly stop using a product such as a website or app if they are having a bad user experience. When users leave a site quickly this is known as having a ‘high bounce rate’.</p>
<p>Bad UI design could also result in security issues, data integrity, and affect the performance of a website.</p>
<p><strong>Chris:</strong> One of the main overarching considerations when creating, is to put the user at the heart of the design. For the user to have a good experience, the UI designer will need to focus on certain elements, such as:</p>
<p>• Placing users in control of the interface</p>
<p>• Making it comfortable to interact with</p>
<p>• Reducing the cognitive load</p>
<p>• Making user interfaces consistent</p>
<p><strong>Leah:</strong> so, what exactly are the Principles of UI design</p>
<p>When it comes to user interface [UI] design there are certain characteristics to consider:</p>
<p>• Clarity – All UI element need to be clear for users to interpretate correctly. Users should not need to decode a particular meaning of an element such as interactive and navigation items.</p>
<p><strong>Chris:</strong> • Consistency –The UI design needs to consistent such as the branding colours and typography. This is so users can recognise ways to use elements such as the placement and colour of a button or menu item is always the same colour, size, font and positioned in the same place.</p>
<p><strong>Leah:</strong> • Ease of use – also categorised as user control – the interface needs to be intuitive and minimises confusion such as using simple navigation, providing feedback [such as a ‘success’ message], and using clear labels for buttons.</p>
<p><strong>Chris:</strong> • Comfort – also categorised as Familiarity this links to clarity and ease of use. The UI should allow for previous experiences of the users, use simple language, include inclusivity, and be designed for responsiveness for use on different items such as a mobile phone, tablet or laptop.</p>
<p><strong>Leah:</strong> • Tolerance – also categorised as Forgiveness – Users needs to be forgiven of their mistakes that they make especially when first using the interface.</p>
<p><strong>Chris:</strong> • Efficiency – the interface should plan for minimal input and provide shortcuts for experienced users so they can be more productive.</p>
<p><strong>Leah:</strong> • Accessibility – this is linked to the Comfort principle as it is key that user feel comfortable and in control when using a product, no matter what their requirements are. UI design needs to take motor, cognitive, hearing, or visual impairments into consideration.</p>
<p><strong>Chris:</strong> Other ‘Principle’ terms that may be used could include Structure, Simplicity, Visibility, and Feedback. This would depend on the type of interface that is going to be designed, and the strategy, processes, or practices of the company or organisation.</p>

          </toggle-section>
        
      
          
